i just want to start by saying  it is always a pleasure to talk to you  i'm a huge fan  i think it's amazing that i get to do  this you know get to talk to you like  this  oh please my pleasure thank you uh i am  curious  i always like asking a few questions  that are not about the movie to start  things off um  when did you feel like you made it as an  actor  that you could do this for a living and  like pay rent doing it  well i'll tell you it was 1975  i did an audition for a lady who's no  longer with us called mary o'malley who  ran built and ran this theater called  the lyric  players theater in belfast and it was  during the height of the  troubles and i auditioned  in order to become a professional actor  and  after my audition i don't think i was  terribly good but i think she saw some  kind of passion  there she sat me down i just  she and i in the auditorium of the lyric  theater  and the more she talked to me about how  hard it is  to be a professional actor the more she  she  she uh said how hard it is to get work  70  or out of work all the time i kept  thinking to myself  she's going to offer me a season  she's going to offer me a part in this  next play  and she did and uh what got me the part  was the fact that i was six foot four  and they needed somebody over six feet  to play this particular character  but anyway i signed a contract uh  in october 1975  to join this theater for a season which  was then doing a play  every four weeks uh the whole european  american calendar  shakespeare arthur miller  you name it and  it was then i i absolutely loved it i  just loved it i loved it i said this is  what i want to do  for the rest of my life you know never  dreaming of film  that was something way way way out there  somewhere  uh the height of my dreams would have  been  to be in the national theater of great  britain maybe the royal shakespeare  company  and that was it but uh doing those plays  every four weeks that was  uh that was the greatest high  i've ever felt i'm glad this worked out  me too so one of the things about ice  road  is that uh when you guys made it it was  an indie movie and then it had this big  sale to netflix  when you were making it um did you have  any idea it was gonna  i guess talk about landing on netflix  and the whole world gets to watch it in  like a week  next flicks bought it and they paid a  lot of money for it  which is great they're a huge streaming  service as we all know  and they do some extraordinary programs  and programming  um but we had made it for a bigger  screen  i just have to say that you know it  wasn't made  specifically for netflix netflix bought  it and  there we go and it releases on june 25th  i believe one of the things about the  film is that i believe you did that  sequence where you go in the water  and like the freezing cold water you  actually went in  on what take did you actually say this  was a really bad idea  no i never did i i i knew it was coming  up  i'd read it in the script i thought okay  they asked me what i want my stunt  double  mark van slow that we've done 25 movies  together  to do it i says no i i want to do this i  i practice a little bit of the wim hof  method i don't know if you know  who wimphoff is this is a guy who  you know climbs halfway out everest up  climbs halfway up everest and a pair of  shorts  and hiking boots and bare chested  and he has these breathing techniques  and he submerges himself in ice cold  water  both my sons practice it i practice  it a little bit and so i knew  i wanted to do it and uh and we did it  we did  maybe three takes certainly no more than  that  and then we had a wonderful hot tub  about 100 meters away off the ice  where we could just get the costume and  all  and thaw out for 20 minutes or half an  hour  uh when i when i spoke to you for the  marksman earlier this year  i mentioned that disney was doing the  star wars series with ewan mcgregor  and i think i was i think i might have  told you this for the first time i don't  i don't know  are you a little surprised at how many  people have been bringing up star wars  with you  um and did you ever get that call from  disney  uh i heard you i'm just doing this  series and i um  uh it's called obi-wan right or ruby one  kenobi  um i haven't been approached  to be honest um i know there's so many  spin-offs my i  i'm a little confused i must admit  but uh and you know when i was in the  first one the phantom menace it was like  oh  would you sign this it's uh it's for my  kid  and now it's like oh would you sign this  it's for my father  or would you sign this it's for my  grandfather i mean it's been  just you know three generations now  since these  movies came out and i remember being in  belfast in the theater  uh having a day off and going to see it  uh the first one i think it was episode  four i believe  with harrison and mark and carrie of  course  and uh in this packed belfast  cinema all ages kids  old people packed and hearing sirens  outside army trucks going by because it  was it was a bad time in northern  ireland let's face it  and uh and seeing this extraordinary  film uh set in outer space  sat in different planets and stuff and  uh it was just totally unique and  incredibly entertaining  never once did i think i might be a part  of it somewhere down the line but  it's still going strong and as i say  that  all these spin-offs and i'm not  surprised uh  there's a spin-off of obi-wan and  newman's there  i look forward to seeing it very much i  really hope you make a guest appearance  i have to go  listen thank you so much for your time  congrats on everything i hope everything  goes well with you  thank you thanks buddy

